# Stable device names for the kobold MCU boards.
#
# Install:  sudo ./scripts/setup-udev.sh
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS: /dev/ttyUSB0 and /dev/ttyUSB1 swap between reboots depending
# on enumeration order. The failure mode is sending motor commands to the
# ultrasonic board, which is exactly as bad as it sounds.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS ROBOT: two identical CP2102 boards, matched on PHYSICAL PORT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read off the hardware 2026-08-04 — both ESP32 DevKit V1 boards present as:
#
#     idVendor  10c4        idProduct ea60
#     manufacturer "Silicon Labs"
#     product      "CP2102 USB to UART Bridge Controller"
#     serial       "0001"          <-- IDENTICAL on both
#
# Cheap CP2102 boards ship with the serial left at its factory default, so there
# is nothing electrically unique to match on. The only stable discriminator is
# which socket the board is plugged into.
#
# CONSEQUENCE, AND IT MATTERS: the role follows the SOCKET, not the board. Move
# a board to the other port and it changes role. Label both the ports and the
# boards, and always use the same one.
#
# This is backstopped in software: the firmware announces its board id in the
# version frame at boot, and kobold_bridge refuses to talk to a board whose id
# is not the one that port is meant to hold. A swap therefore produces a loud
# refusal rather than motor commands going somewhere dangerous.
#
# If you would rather identity followed the board than the socket, the CP2102
# serial is rewritable (cp210x-cfg). Then match ATTRS{serial} — see the bottom
# of this file — and drop the KERNELS clauses.
#
# Find your port paths with:
#   for d in /dev/ttyUSB*; do echo "$d $(udevadm info -q path -n $d)"; done
#
# Common bridge chips:
#   1a86:7523  CH340          (most DevKit V1 clones)
#   1a86:55d4  CH9102 / CH343 (many USB-C boards)
#   10c4:ea60  CP2102         (Silicon Labs; serial is often left at "0001")

# ---- drive board: motors, encoders, IMU, cliff reflex ----
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", KERNELS=="3-1.1", SYMLINK+="robot-drive", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- sense board: ultrasonic, IR, buzzer, OLED, safety line ----
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", KERNELS=="3-1.2", SYMLINK+="robot-sense", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- head board (Phase 5+): pan/tilt servos, PIR, second OLED ----
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", KERNELS=="3-1.3", SYMLINK+="robot-head", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- lidar (Phase 5, if ever fitted) ----
# DELIBERATELY NOT a bare 10c4:ea60 match. An earlier revision of this file had
# exactly that, and because these ESP32 boards use the same CP2102 bridge it
# grabbed them both: /dev/robot-lidar pointed at an ESP32, and the second board
# silently overwrote the first one's symlink. Uncomment with a real port path
# only once a lidar physically exists.
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", KERNELS=="3-1.4", SYMLINK+="robot-lidar", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"

# ---- boards with genuinely unique serials ----
# If you reflash the CP2102 serials, prefer these and delete the port matching
# above — identity then follows the board into any socket.
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="KOBOLD-DRIVE", SYMLINK+="robot-drive", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"
#SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="10c4", ATTRS{idProduct}=="ea60", ATTRS{serial}=="KOBOLD-SENSE", SYMLINK+="robot-sense", MODE="0660", GROUP="dialout"
